ing a reference  point so I knew that no matter what I  had a future that I was gonna be okay  that even in my community even if I  didn't see those role models for those  people thriving I knew that they were  out there somewhere I just had to get  out there I finally got to a point in my  relationship where I knew we weren't  gonna get anywhere until I came out  until we both came out and it's gonna  suck and it might hurt and it might be  really detrimental to some relationships  in our lives but it's gonna be better in  the long run I was in a safe space I was  in a safe environment where I was able  to come out  and I was fortunate enough to have a mom  who listened when I come up to my mom  she took it the hardest out of everyone  but I felt like I had the resources to  help her understand what it was and what  it meant and help her through this  process because I understand that for  her is probably really hard so we were  actually on a long like a two-hour ish  car ride home you know I really need to  get this off my chest everything I  pretty much thought she would think and  say she did and in points it got even  kind of worse than what I thought it  would get it was pretty devastating like  it's the kind of stuff that you hear in  media all the time in on TV of like you  know these extreme stories about like  families kicking their kids out for  being gay or just like kind of saying  these really awful things to hear that  coming out of your own mom's mouth it  was almost a surreal experience even  though it didn't go the way I was hoping  it still helped me grow as a person and  I wouldn't trade it for the world  I can't imagine my adult life without  coming out I can't imagine if I was  still that person that I was trying to  pretend to be and I think a difference  between then versus now is that I'm my  full self now I finally get to really  figure out who I am as a person  and who I'm gonna be as a person for the  rest of my life like being gay isn't 